  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Benchmark Peak</strong> is a 3,186-foot summit in Alaska. The Benchmark Peak Trail covers 13.7 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 656 feet of elevation gain \u2014 gentle grades, but the mileage is what makes it a Hard outing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=59.98698,-149.39047\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">59.98698, -149.39047</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Hard rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 5.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Race Point, Andy Simons Mountain and Axis Peak \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
